# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files — secrets never committed; the documented template is the exception
.env*
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
e2e/.auth/
test-results/
playwright-report/

# impeccable tool artefacts.
#
# The written records are kept — the surface brief, the comp approval sidecars
# and their prompts are design documentation and belong in the repo. What is
# ignored is the generated weight: review screenshots and the decision comps
# run to ~22MB of PNG that nothing reads twice, and session journals and the
# hook cache are machine state.
.impeccable/review/
.impeccable/mocks/*.png
.impeccable/live/sessions/
.impeccable/hook.cache.json

# Local machine state, not a shared setting.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Installed agent skills — a vendored tree, not source.
#
# `.agents/skills/` and `.claude/skills/` hold third-party skill packages
# fetched from GitHub (1.2MB and growing). They are `node_modules` by another
# name: nothing here imports them, nothing here builds without them, and they
# belong to whoever is driving the editor rather than to Fairfame.
#
# `skills-lock.json` is their lockfile and goes with them. Normally a lockfile
# is committed precisely so the vendored tree can be rebuilt — that rule is for
# dependencies the *project* needs, and these are personal tooling for image
# generation and brand kits with no bearing on this codebase. Committing a lock
# for packages the build never touches would oblige every clone to reason about
# them. Delete these three lines if the team ever adopts a shared skill set.
#
# **Only `skills/` is ignored under `.claude/`, not the directory.** The line
# above already treats `settings.local.json` as the local half, which means
# `.claude/settings.json` and any shared commands are meant to be committable.
# Ignoring the whole folder would quietly reverse that.
.claude/skills/
.agents/
skills-lock.json

# Editor-local configuration.
#
# `.vscode/` here holds one personal MCP server pointing at a Sentry endpoint —
# a preference about somebody's editor, not a fact about the project. Committed
# `.vscode/extensions.json` is a reasonable thing for a team to want later; add
# a `!` exception for it rather than removing this line.
.vscode/
.idea/

# Sentry Config File
.env.sentry-build-plugin
